

00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:34.237
<v Girl> Hi Coleman, how are you?

00:01:34.237 --> 00:01:35.546
<v Coleman> Good thanks.

00:01:35.546 --> 00:01:44.703
<v Girl> I wanted to have a chat with you because our friend Josh told me that you've joined a guitar group and it sounds interesting.

00:01:44.703 --> 00:01:47.411
<v Girl> I'd really like to learn myself.

00:01:47.411 --> 00:01:51.942
<v Coleman>Why don't you come along? I'm sure there's room for another person.

00:01:51.942 --> 00:01:53.251
<v Girl> Really?

00:01:53.251 --> 00:01:56.347
<v Girl> So, who runs the classes?

00:01:56.347 --> 00:02:02.234
<v Coleman> He's called a coordinator. His name... Gary Matheson.

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:08.557
<v Girl> Let me note that down. Gary, how do you spell his surname?

00:02:08.557 --> 00:02:19.503
<v Coleman> It's M -A -T -H -I -E -S -O -N.

00:02:19.503 --> 00:02:21.470
<v Girl> Right, thanks.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:29.664
<v Coleman> He's retired, actually, but he's a really nice guy and he used to play in a lot of bands.

00:02:29.664 --> 00:02:34.591
<v Girl> Thanks. So how long have you been going?

00:02:34.591 --> 00:02:36.772
<v Coleman> About a month now.

00:02:36.772 --> 00:02:40.609
<v Girl> And could you play anything before you started?

00:02:40.609 --> 00:02:43.880
<v Coleman> I knew a few chords, but that's all.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.889
<v Girl> I'm sure everyone will be better than me.

00:02:46.889 --> 00:02:49.157
<v Coleman> That's what I thought too.

00:02:49.157 --> 00:02:58.794
<v Coleman> When I first spoke to Gary on the phone, he said it was a class for beginners, but I was still worried that everyone would be better than me.

00:02:58.794 --> 00:03:02.021
<v Coleman> But we were all equally hopeless.

00:03:02.021 --> 00:03:04.638
<v Girl> Oh, that's reassuring.

00:03:04.638 --> 00:03:07.080
<v Girl> So, where do you meet?

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:18.123
<v Coleman> Well, when I joined the group, they were meeting in Gary's home. But as the group got bigger, he decided to book a room at the college in town.

00:03:18.123 --> 00:03:20.249
<v Coleman> I prefer going there.

00:03:20.249 --> 00:03:22.343
<v Girl> I know that place.

00:03:22.343 --> 00:03:34.055
<v Girl> I used to go to tap dancing classes there when I was at secondary school. I haven't been since, though, and I can't remember what road it's in.

00:03:34.055 --> 00:03:36.515
<v Girl> Is it Lock Street?

00:03:36.515 --> 00:03:43.231
<v Coleman> It's just beyond there, at the bottom of New Street, near the city roundabout.

00:03:43.231 --> 00:03:45.455
<v Girl> Yes, of course.

00:03:45.455 --> 00:03:52.214
<v Coleman> The guitar club is on the first floor in room T347.

00:03:52.258 --> 00:03:53.784
<v Girl> Right.

00:03:53.784 --> 00:03:58.450
<v Girl> And when do you meet? Is it at the weekend?

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.067
<v Coleman> We meet on Thursdays.

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:05.602
<v Coleman> It used to be 10.30, and that suited me well.

00:04:05.689 --> 00:04:09.309
<v Coleman> But now, we meet at 11.

00:04:09.309 --> 00:04:15.327
<v Coleman> The class that's in there before us asked if they could have the room for another 30 minutes.

00:04:15.327 --> 00:04:17.464
<v Girl> Oh, I see.

00:04:17.464 --> 00:04:22.915
<v Girl> Well, I'd love to come, but I don't have a guitar.

00:04:22.915 --> 00:04:26.838
<v Coleman> Well, you can always buy a secondhand one.

00:04:26.838 --> 00:04:35.518
<v Coleman> There's a website called The Perfect Instrument that sells all kinds of guitars, violins, and so on.

00:04:35.518 --> 00:04:38.003
<v Coleman> I'm sure you'll find something there.

00:05:13.937 --> 00:05:18.603
<v Girl> So, what's a typical lesson like with Gary?

00:05:18.603 --> 00:05:23.879
<v Coleman> Well, he always starts by getting us to tune our guitars.

00:05:23.879 --> 00:05:26.409
<v Coleman> That takes about five minutes.

00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:27.630
<v Girl> Uh-huh.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:33.386
<v Coleman> Some people have an app they use, but others do it by ear.

00:05:33.386 --> 00:05:36.220
<v Coleman> Gary goes round and helps them.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:42.020
<v Coleman> And, while he's doing that, he tells us what he's going to do during the lesson. 

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.656
<v Girl> Right.

00:05:43.656 --> 00:05:49.717
<v Coleman> First, we usually spend about ten minutes doing some strumming.

00:05:49.717 --> 00:05:55.561
<v Girl> So, is that using...oh, what are they called?

00:05:55.561 --> 00:05:57.523
<v Girl> Plectrums?

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:00.532
<v Coleman> No, we just use our thumbs.

00:06:00.532 --> 00:06:02.887
<v Girl> Oh, much easier.

00:06:02.887 --> 00:06:09.734
<v Coleman> Gary reminds us where to put our fingers for each chord, and then we play them together.

00:06:09.734 --> 00:06:16.057
<v Coleman> Sometimes we all just start laughing, because we're so bad at keeping time.

00:06:16.057 --> 00:06:19.829
<v Coleman> So Gary starts clapping to help us.

00:06:19.829 --> 00:06:22.620
<v Girl> Do you learn to play any songs?

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:31.123
<v Coleman> Yes, we do at least one song with words and chords. I mean, that's harder than you think.

00:06:31.123 --> 00:06:33.522
<v Girl> Oh, I'm sure it is.

00:06:33.522 --> 00:06:38.406
<v Coleman> That part of the lesson takes about fifteen minutes.

00:06:38.406 --> 00:06:55.413
<v Coleman> He often brings a recording of the song, and plays it to us first. Then he hands out the song, and if there's a new chord in it, we practice that before we play it together, but really slowly.

00:06:55.413 --> 00:06:58.248
<v Girl> Do you do any finger -picking?

00:06:58.248 --> 00:07:06.505
<v Coleman> That's the last ten minutes of the lesson, when we pick out the individual notes from a tune he's made up.

00:07:06.505 --> 00:07:08.845
<v Coleman> It's always quite simple.

00:07:08.845 --> 00:07:10.647
<v Girl> That must be hard, though.

00:07:10.647 --> 00:07:16.738
<v Coleman> It is. But people like it, because they can really concentrate.

00:07:16.738 --> 00:07:21.927
<v Coleman> And if we're all playing well, it sounds quite impressive.

00:07:21.927 --> 00:07:27.509
<v Coleman> The only trouble is that he sometimes gets us to play one at a time.

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:29.883
<v Coleman> You know, alone.

00:07:29.883 --> 00:07:31.870
<v Girl> Oh, that's scary.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:36.623
<v Coleman> Mmm, it is. But I've got used to it now.

00:07:36.623 --> 00:07:43.143
<v Coleman> At the end, he spends about five minutes telling us what to practice for the following week.

00:07:43.143 --> 00:07:49.562
<v Girl> Well, thanks Coleman. I'll go and have a look at that website, I think.
